      s1Title: 'A new town laid out in 1588',
      s1Body: '<p>The district takes its name from Duke Charles III of Lorraine, who had the Ville-Neuve laid out here in 1588 to relieve the medieval old town, by then packed behind its walls. Hence the plan: straight streets, regular blocks, a geometry you spot the moment you look at a map of Nancy.</p><p>Two gates of that wall are still standing, Porte Saint-Nicolas and Porte Saint-Georges. Place Stanislas came two centuries later, on the hinge between the new town and the old one. That is why it is a six-minute walk from Le 1901 rather than across town. Since not one of the square’s four sides holds an apartment, [[page:stanislas|the closest you can sleep to it]] is right here.</p>',
      s2Title: 'What you find downstairs',
      s2Body: '<p>The covered market hall is 150 m away, a two-minute walk: cheesemongers, greengrocers, fishmongers, butchers. It does not open every day, so check the day’s hours before counting on it for dinner. The tables in the district are listed in [[home:#guide|our address book]].</p><p>Around it, Rue Saint-Dizier and Rue des Ponts form Nancy’s main shopping artery, with its bakeries, cafés and the Saint-Sébastien shopping centre. The church of Saint-Sébastien, with its 18th-century baroque front, closes the square next to the hall.</p><p>For cars, the Saint-Dizier and Dom Calmet car parks are under three minutes away. On-street parking is paid during the day and rarely free: plan for the car park from the first evening.</p>',
